<div xmlns:perseus="http://www.perseus.org/meta/perseus.rdfs#" xmlns:dctype="http://purl.org/dc/dcmitype/" xmlns:tufts="http://www.tufts.edu/" xmlns:alph="http://alpheios.net/namespaces/tei" class="alpheios-lex-text_container en">
    <div class="alpheios-lex-entry" lemma-id="n20473" body-key=""><b><span class="alpheios-lex-la" lang="lat">Hēraclīdes</span></b> ,
			ae, m., <div class="alpheios-lex-lex_sense alpheios-lex-lex_sense1"><b>I.</b>
			<i>a proper name.</i>
		</div>
        <div class="alpheios-lex-lex_sense alpheios-lex-lex_sense1"><b>I.</b>
			<i>A physician</i>, <i><span class="en">Cels. 3,
			6</span></i>.—</div>
        <div class="alpheios-lex-lex_sense alpheios-lex-lex_sense1"><b>II.</b>
			Called Ponticus, <i>a Greek philosopher of Heraclea in
				Pontus</i>, <i>a pupil of Plato and
			Speusippus</i>, <i>and afterwards of Aristotle</i>,
				<i><span class="en">Cic.
				Leg. 3, 6</span></i>; <i><span class="en">id. Tusc. 5, 3, 8
			al.</span></i>—</div>
        <div class="alpheios-lex-lex_sense alpheios-lex-lex_sense1"><b>III.</b>
			Heraclides Ponticus the younger, <i>a grammarian</i>,
			Gell. ap. <i><span class="en">Prisc. 705</span></i>
			P.—</div>
        <div class="alpheios-lex-lex_sense alpheios-lex-lex_sense1"><b>IV.</b>
			<i>A painter</i>, <i><span class="en">Plin. 35,
				11, 40, § 135</span></i>.</div>
    </div> <div class="alpheios-lex-footnotes en"> </div>
    <div class="alpheios-lex-alph-source">From <u>
            <u>A Latin Dictionary</u>
        </u> (Charlton T. Lewis, Charles Short, Oxford, Clarendon Press, 1879) </div>
</div>